A use restriction accompanies this licence and is stated as a normative
condition rather than a legal one.

These 28,800 assessments are unsafe as synthetic epidemiological data, which is
what the paper's results establish. The corpus is released for auditing,
replication and method development, and not for training, for synthetic
prevalence estimation, or for inclusion in any downstream clinical dataset.
Using it for those purposes would propagate exactly the miscalibration the
paper documents.

The non-commercial and no-derivatives terms above have force. The rest of that
restriction does not: no licence we are aware of can distinguish auditing from
training as downstream uses, and we would rather say so than imply an
enforceability the document does not have.
